---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import Comparison from '../components/Comparison.astro';
import DeployButton from '../components/DeployButton.astro';
const sections=[{id:'what',label:'What you get'},{id:'deploy',label:'Deploy'},{id:'cost',label:'What it costs'},{id:'how',label:'How it works'},{id:'limits',label:'Good to know'}];
---
<Page title="Self-host on Cloudflare" description="Run Slurp in your own Cloudflare account: a Worker, Durable Objects and an R2 bucket. Often free to start, with a price comparison to hosted analytics." current="self-host" sections={sections}>
<header style="padding-bottom:40px"><p class="eyebrow">Self-host</p><h1>Your events.<br/><span class="hot">Your Cloudflare account.</span></h1><p class="lede">Slurp runs as one Worker, two Durable Object classes and an R2 bucket in your own Cloudflare account. No servers to run, no data leaving your account, and the Free plan is enough to start.</p><div class="actions"><DeployButton id="hero"/><a class="btn" href="/docs/">Read the quickstart</a></div><p class="fine">Open source · MIT · Deploys with one command</p></header>
<section class="section" id="what"><span class="section-number">01 / WHAT YOU GET</span><h2>The whole pipeline, in your account.</h2>
<div class="steps"><div><h3><span class="hot">1.</span> Capture</h3><p>Your app sends events with the SDK or plain HTTP to your Worker. Each one is validated, stamped with arrival time and, if you allow it, the visitor's country, region and city from Cloudflare.</p></div><div><h3><span class="hot">2.</span> Store</h3><p>Events are buffered durably, then sealed into compressed archives in your R2 bucket. Archives are immutable and checksummed.</p></div><div><h3><span class="hot">3.</span> Ask</h3><p>The CLI downloads new archives into a DuckDB file on your machine. Your coding agent queries it with SQL and builds reports.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="deploy"><span class="section-number">02 / DEPLOY</span><h2>One command.</h2><p><code>slurp cloud init</code> logs in to Cloudflare, creates the R2 bucket, deploys the Worker with its Durable Objects, sets a generated admin token and connects your computer. No checkout of the Worker source and no <code>npm install</code>: the Worker ships inside the CLI.</p><div class="actions"><DeployButton id="deploy"/></div><Code label="Terminal" code={`slurp cloud init
slurp setup
slurp init --name "My app"
slurp send signed_up --user-id demo --data '{"demo":true}'
slurp sync`}/><p class="fine">Check on it any time with <code>slurp cloud status</code> or <code>slurp cloud doctor</code>. <code>slurp cloud upgrade</code> redeploys a newer Worker and keeps your data. <code>slurp cloud destroy</code> removes everything.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="cost"><span class="section-number">03 / WHAT IT COSTS</span><h2>Cents to start. A few hundred to a few thousand at 10 billion.</h2><p>You pay Cloudflare directly, at list price, for what your Worker uses. Most small and medium apps fit inside the Workers Free plan. Past that, Workers Paid is $5 a month plus usage. Hosted analytics tools bill by event volume, so the gap widens as you grow.</p><Comparison/></section>
<section class="section" id="how"><span class="section-number">04 / HOW IT WORKS</span><h2>Built from Cloudflare primitives that last.</h2><ul><li><strong>One Durable Object per source</strong> groups concurrent requests into one write, acknowledges only after the data is durable, and seals about every 10 minutes (or at 8 MB) into <code>jsonl.gz</code> archives with a gapless sequence and a SHA-256 for each.</li><li><strong>R2</strong> keeps the archives. The CLI never gets R2 credentials: it asks your Worker for short-lived signed download links.</li><li><strong>The CLI</strong> verifies every archive, imports it into DuckDB idempotently, and remembers where it stopped, so retries and overlaps never duplicate events.</li><li><strong>Keys</strong> come in two kinds: publishable keys can only send events (safe in a browser), secret keys can only read one source's archives.</li></ul></section>
<section class="section" id="limits"><span class="section-number">05 / GOOD TO KNOW</span><h2>Honest limits.</h2><ul><li><strong>Freshness.</strong> New events show up in <code>slurp sync</code> after the next seal, about 10 minutes by default. Change it with <code>SEAL_INTERVAL_MS</code>.</li><li><strong>Free plan cap.</strong> Cloudflare limits Free Workers and Durable Objects to 100,000 requests a day. At 20 events per request that is roughly 2M events a day. If you hit it, the SDK keeps events and retries; upgrade to Paid to lift it.</li><li><strong>Retention.</strong> Archives stay in R2 until you delete them. Set <code>RETENTION_DAYS</code> to expire old ones.</li><li><strong>Security.</strong> Anyone with your admin token controls the deployment. Store it in a password manager, and create a separate read key for each bot.</li><li><strong>No accounts or billing.</strong> There are no logins, plans or invoices from Slurp. The admin token is the only credential.</li></ul></section>
</Page>
